WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.820
Two cells in the mouse olfactory bulb take their input from one glomerulus.

2
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:14.900
A glomerulus is where receptor neurons converge, and cells that share one are called

3
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:18.120
sisters. Do sisters send the same message?

4
00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.220
If so, the glomerulus is the message.

5
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:24.100
Or is it only an address that they share?

6
00:00:24.933 --> 00:00:29.193
Mitral and tufted cells are the output neurons of the mouse bulb.

7
00:00:29.873 --> 00:00:33.413
Each sends one primary dendrite into one glomerulus.

8
00:00:34.613 --> 00:00:37.153
Cells that share a glomerulus are sister cells.

9
00:00:38.273 --> 00:00:43.333
Dhawale and colleagues recorded sisters in adult anaesthetized mice, and used light

10
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:46.053
to find which cells shared a parent glomerulus.

11
00:00:46.813 --> 00:00:50.973
So sisters were defined by their input, not by how they responded to odours.

12
00:00:51.867 --> 00:00:56.387
First, they summarized each response by counting mean spikes.

13
00:00:57.107 --> 00:00:58.947
That gives a rate for each odour.

14
00:01:00.207 --> 00:01:03.407
Across odours, the rates of the two sisters were correlated.

15
00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:07.347
When one sister fired more for an odour, so did the other.

16
00:01:08.127 --> 00:01:11.147
By rate, the sisters looked like copies of each other.

17
00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:16.590
Next, they looked at spike timing against the respiration cycle.

18
00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.270
Each breath is one cycle.

19
00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:21.910
That is the rhythm of the animal's breathing.

20
00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:25.790
Odours changed the timing of the sisters differently.

21
00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:30.650
The same number of spikes can fall at different moments in the breath.

22
00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:34.030
Timing did not treat the sisters like copies.

23
00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:40.320
Both statements belong to the result, and neither is a retreat from the other.

24
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.860
Around the output cells sit interneurons such as granule cells.

25
00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:51.380
Their reciprocal synapses let a cell inhibit itself and its neighbours.

26
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:57.580
But timing differences do not show which synapses made them, so they do not show

27
00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:59.500
that granule cell inhibition did.

28
00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:05.760
And the mice were anaesthetized, so an awake, sniffing mouse is a separate question.

29
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:10.680
So a shared glomerulus is a shared address.

30
00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.460
It is not a shared message.

31
00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:21.020
Rate alone treats sisters as copies, so a rate-only reading can miss part of the

32
00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:26.860
output. Timing against the breath shows that sisters are not copies.

33
00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:31.760
Sisters can agree on how much, and disagree on when.
